  "answer": "The supplied passages do not establish that a country name always requires a limitation. They also do not explain when a limitation would be required or sufficient.\n\nManual practice instead calls for a contextual assessment:\n• Does the name convey geographical significance for the claimed goods or services, and would other traders want to use it for that significance? [TMM/Part22/15#1]\n• A place name does not necessarily signify that location when used as a trade mark. [TMM/Part22/15/15/1~1]\n• Conversely, words functioning solely as geographical names, with an obvious or potential connection to the goods or services, have no inherent adaptation to distinguish. [TMM/Part22/15/15/3]\n\nAccordingly, these passages support assessing the name in relation to the goods or services—not assuming an automatic limitation requirement. I cannot determine the applicable limitation requirements from the supplied material. These are statements of Manual practice; no Act or Regulations passages were provided."
